WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.396
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.396 --> 00:00:09.420
ELISSE ROCHE: Hello, everyone,
and welcome to the "Data

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:12.420
for Good" session on driving
social and environmental impact

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:14.100
with data analytics.

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:16.830
My name is Elisse Roche,
and I lead our initiatives

00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:19.260
around data for social
and environmental impact

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:20.463
here at Google Cloud.

00:00:20.463 --> 00:00:21.880
ALONSO ORTIZ GALAN:
Thanks, Elise.

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:23.480
Thanks, everyone,
for being here.

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:26.470
I'm Alonso Ortiz, the
community engagement manager

00:00:26.470 --> 00:00:29.860
for the Global Partnership for
Sustainable Development Data.

00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:31.930
Global Partnership is
a network committed

00:00:31.930 --> 00:00:34.925
to ensure we have the necessary
data to achieve DSDGs.

00:00:34.925 --> 00:00:37.050
ELISSE ROCHE: And what
we're really looking forward

00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:40.140
to discussing here today
are the opportunities

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:42.390
to apply data analytics
and machine-learning

00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:46.200
to a variety of issues and to
solving some of the world's

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:48.102
greatest challenges.

00:00:48.102 --> 00:00:50.310
In addition to that, also
speaking to some of the use

00:00:50.310 --> 00:00:52.560
cases that we're currently
seeing with non-profits who

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:54.390
are using our
solutions, and also

00:00:54.390 --> 00:00:57.180
an initiative that we
collaborate on together

00:00:57.180 --> 00:01:00.198
around data visualization.

00:01:00.198 --> 00:01:02.240
So, we thought before
digging a little bit deeper

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:04.769
into what Google Cloud
is doing in this space

00:01:04.769 --> 00:01:07.730
and what the Global Partnership
team is doing in this space,

00:01:07.730 --> 00:01:11.930
wanted to look at Data for Good
as a concept, as a movement,

00:01:11.930 --> 00:01:15.683
and discuss how it
has evolved over time.

00:01:15.683 --> 00:01:17.600
So, Data for Good is an
international movement

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:19.220
where private
companies are working

00:01:19.220 --> 00:01:22.072
with governmental agencies,
NGOs, and nonprofits

00:01:22.072 --> 00:01:24.530
to apply big data solutions to
some of the world's greatest

00:01:24.530 --> 00:01:25.550
challenges.

00:01:25.550 --> 00:01:30.050
And, dating back to
example four in 2008,

00:01:30.050 --> 00:01:33.130
the CDC collaborated with Google
to launch Google Flu Trends,

00:01:33.130 --> 00:01:35.900
with this idea that you could
actually look at search trends

00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:38.810
around flu symptoms--
or similar searches

00:01:38.810 --> 00:01:42.500
to actually help predict
outbreaks in that area.

00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:46.070
In 2011, the United Nations
Global Pulse Organization

00:01:46.070 --> 00:01:48.170
advocated for this idea
of data philanthropy

00:01:48.170 --> 00:01:50.390
where organizations
that have access to data

00:01:50.390 --> 00:01:54.110
can help make it available
for public benefit.

00:01:54.110 --> 00:01:57.200
In 2013, the United Nations
also utilized satellite imagery

00:01:57.200 --> 00:01:59.150
to provide targeted
disaster relief,

00:01:59.150 --> 00:02:02.570
looking at pre-disaster
and post-disaster areas

00:02:02.570 --> 00:02:06.290
in order to determine which
areas, communites, et cetera

00:02:06.290 --> 00:02:10.160
needed the most support
as soon as possible.

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:14.570
Later in 2017, the UN hosted the
world's first World Data Forum,

00:02:14.570 --> 00:02:18.350
the second of which
actually took place in 2018,

00:02:18.350 --> 00:02:20.840
bringing together a variety
of different industries

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:23.360
to discuss, again, how we
can all better work together

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:24.920
across the private
and public sectors

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:29.010
in order to apply data analytics
to these challenging issues.

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:32.510
And, here we are in 2018 looking
at interdisciplinary programs

00:02:32.510 --> 00:02:34.040
in collaboration.

00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:36.080
And, with that, there's
the question-- so,

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:40.130
what exactly is Google
Cloud doing in this space?

00:02:40.130 --> 00:02:42.430
So, Google Cloud--
here, we are working

00:02:42.430 --> 00:02:45.610
to empower nonprofits
with integrated solutions

00:02:45.610 --> 00:02:49.810
from geospatial analysis tools
to productivity tools to even

00:02:49.810 --> 00:02:53.350
a nonprofit grant program.

00:02:53.350 --> 00:02:56.860
In 2007, Google Maps helped
nonprofits access satellite

00:02:56.860 --> 00:03:01.290
imagery and APIs with
Google Earth outreach.

00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:04.300
In 2011, G-Suite for
nonprofits launched

00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:06.640
with the idea of enabling
nonprofits to collaborate

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:08.090
and innovate together.

00:03:08.090 --> 00:03:10.390
It's also worth noting that
those two offerings are

00:03:10.390 --> 00:03:13.110
currently available through the
Google for Nonprofits website

00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:15.600
and platform.

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:18.470
In 2016, we launched
GCP public data

00:03:18.470 --> 00:03:21.640
sets with this idea of
democratizing access

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:23.440
to planetary scale
data sets that are

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:25.330
freely hosted in the cloud.

00:03:25.330 --> 00:03:27.580
So, whether you're a
non-profit organization or not,

00:03:27.580 --> 00:03:31.670
really any GCP user can
access these public data

00:03:31.670 --> 00:03:34.720
sets and analyze them up
to one terabyte per month

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:35.930
at no charge.

00:03:35.930 --> 00:03:38.050
And these include data
sets from organizations

00:03:38.050 --> 00:03:41.710
like the EPA, the US Department
of Health and Human Services,

00:03:41.710 --> 00:03:43.660
the World Bank, the
United Nations Statistics

00:03:43.660 --> 00:03:47.460
Division-- the list continues.

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:50.220
And, in 2018, we actually
announced the Data Solutions

00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:51.510
for Change program.

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:54.060
It's a nonprofit grant
program with the goal

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:56.760
of empowering nonprofits to
achieve their missions at scale

00:03:56.760 --> 00:04:00.100
with data analytics.

00:04:00.100 --> 00:04:02.650
Essentially, we understand
that with big data solutions,

00:04:02.650 --> 00:04:04.733
nonprofits can scale their data.

00:04:04.733 --> 00:04:06.150
They can analyze
data at the scale

00:04:06.150 --> 00:04:08.310
of the entire web with a query.

00:04:08.310 --> 00:04:09.970
They can automate
their work and tap

00:04:09.970 --> 00:04:12.740
into artificial intelligence
with cloud machine-learning.

00:04:12.740 --> 00:04:14.620
And they can also
share their vision

00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:17.440
and turn their data into
customizable reports

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:20.930
with tools like
Google Data Studio.

00:04:20.930 --> 00:04:24.075
And that's, essentially, what
underscores the Data Solutions

00:04:24.075 --> 00:04:25.700
for Change program,
where we're looking

00:04:25.700 --> 00:04:28.670
to address two key challenges
that nonprofits often

00:04:28.670 --> 00:04:32.410
face when trying to access data
analytics and data analytics

00:04:32.410 --> 00:04:35.622
solutions-- one being financial
and the other technical.

00:04:35.622 --> 00:04:37.580
So, with the Data Solutions
for Change program,

00:04:37.580 --> 00:04:40.580
what we're offering
includes Google credits--

00:04:40.580 --> 00:04:42.260
a need-based Google
Cloud credit grant

00:04:42.260 --> 00:04:44.480
depending on their
proposal-- self-training

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:46.970
resources through
Qwiklabs, and also

00:04:46.970 --> 00:04:49.640
hands-on support through
Google Cloud customer

00:04:49.640 --> 00:04:53.790
support for the duration of
their six-month grant period.

00:04:53.790 --> 00:04:56.790
And, while this program
just launched in 2018,

00:04:56.790 --> 00:04:59.340
we're already seeing a
variety of nonprofits

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:04.350
applying these solutions
in really interesting ways.

00:05:04.350 --> 00:05:06.860
One of them is the Open
Agriculture Foundation,

00:05:06.860 --> 00:05:09.290
where they're using BigQuery
and IoT Core in order

00:05:09.290 --> 00:05:11.780
to create healthier, more
engaging, and more inventive

00:05:11.780 --> 00:05:13.190
food with technology.

00:05:13.190 --> 00:05:15.890
They've actually developed
these proprietary food computers

00:05:15.890 --> 00:05:18.170
where they're looking
to, essentially,

00:05:18.170 --> 00:05:19.790
develop plant codes--

00:05:19.790 --> 00:05:22.130
plant recipe codes and
looking at different variables

00:05:22.130 --> 00:05:24.230
so that they can
understand, for example, how

00:05:24.230 --> 00:05:26.840
a basil plant responds
to a certain humidity

00:05:26.840 --> 00:05:31.040
level versus lettuce.

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:32.830
Harambee Youth
Employment Accelerator

00:05:32.830 --> 00:05:36.290
is a nonprofit organization
based in South Africa.

00:05:36.290 --> 00:05:38.200
They're using BigQuery
and machine-learning

00:05:38.200 --> 00:05:42.100
to connect more unemployed
youth in South Africa with jobs.

00:05:42.100 --> 00:05:44.080
They understand that the
public transportation

00:05:44.080 --> 00:05:47.000
system in South Africa can
be particularly challenging.

00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:48.850
And so, with entry
level applicants,

00:05:48.850 --> 00:05:52.690
they're looking at developing
a proprietary machine-learning

00:05:52.690 --> 00:05:55.390
algorithm and a
job-matching algorithm

00:05:55.390 --> 00:05:58.060
so that they're not only
matching these entry level

00:05:58.060 --> 00:06:00.430
applicants with new
positions, but also

00:06:00.430 --> 00:06:02.262
working towards job retention.

00:06:02.262 --> 00:06:03.970
It's also worth noting
that they recently

00:06:03.970 --> 00:06:06.430
received a social
entrepreneurship

00:06:06.430 --> 00:06:07.780
award this year.

00:06:10.308 --> 00:06:11.850
The Foundation for
Precision Medicine

00:06:11.850 --> 00:06:13.530
is using BigQuery
and machine-learning

00:06:13.530 --> 00:06:16.950
to detect Alzheimer's disease
early with the goal of stopping

00:06:16.950 --> 00:06:18.642
the progression of the disease.

00:06:18.642 --> 00:06:20.100
So, with Alzheimer's
in particular,

00:06:20.100 --> 00:06:21.600
there's currently
not a cure today.

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:23.100
And so, a lot of
research is focused

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:24.550
around finding that cure.

00:06:24.550 --> 00:06:27.150
But, what the Foundation for
Precision Medicine is doing is

00:06:27.150 --> 00:06:29.400
they're actually taking a
bit of a different approach.

00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:31.290
They have this idea
of, essentially,

00:06:31.290 --> 00:06:34.860
getting ahead of a diagnosis-- a
traditional diagnosis by months

00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:39.830
or even years so as to prescribe
preventative treatment.

00:06:39.830 --> 00:06:42.463
Power Poetry-- a bit of a
shift towards the arts--

00:06:42.463 --> 00:06:44.630
what they're doing is they're
using machine-learning

00:06:44.630 --> 00:06:47.150
to increase teen literacy
through interactive

00:06:47.150 --> 00:06:48.243
recommendation engines.

00:06:48.243 --> 00:06:50.660
So, they've created their own
proprietary machine-learning

00:06:50.660 --> 00:06:54.470
algorithms to match teen
submissions teen poetry

00:06:54.470 --> 00:06:57.515
submissions with rap
artists and American poets.

00:07:00.330 --> 00:07:02.310
BroodMinder is also
another organization

00:07:02.310 --> 00:07:03.510
working with our technology.

00:07:03.510 --> 00:07:04.885
And what they're
doing is they're

00:07:04.885 --> 00:07:06.810
using Firebase and
Cloud Firestore

00:07:06.810 --> 00:07:10.020
to manage their IoT bee
high-tracking sensors

00:07:10.020 --> 00:07:12.240
and to improve bee
health around the US.

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:15.120
So, for those unfamiliar with
the state of the bee population

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:17.980
today, it is declining--
their health is declining.

00:07:17.980 --> 00:07:20.790
They're facing hive collapse
around the United States.

00:07:20.790 --> 00:07:23.130
And this is concerning not
only to the bee populations

00:07:23.130 --> 00:07:25.350
and biodiversity more
broadly, but also

00:07:25.350 --> 00:07:28.890
given the impact that that
may have upon our food

00:07:28.890 --> 00:07:31.740
systems given the
importance of pollinators,

00:07:31.740 --> 00:07:36.560
especially around
fruits and vegetables.

00:07:36.560 --> 00:07:39.020
And one more example
to share with you

00:07:39.020 --> 00:07:40.800
all is around biodiversity.

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:42.620
So, the Zoological
Society of London

00:07:42.620 --> 00:07:45.968
is using AutoML to optimize
camera trap surveys

00:07:45.968 --> 00:07:48.260
and to advance research
studies with that ultimate goal

00:07:48.260 --> 00:07:49.820
of preserving biodiversity.

00:07:49.820 --> 00:07:52.730
Camera trap surveys are an
extremely popular method

00:07:52.730 --> 00:07:56.030
of research in this space,
and they use motion sensors.

00:07:56.030 --> 00:07:59.580
Oftentimes, these sensors
can have false positives.

00:07:59.580 --> 00:08:02.540
So that what you end up
with after these surveys

00:08:02.540 --> 00:08:05.060
are thousands upon
thousands of images that

00:08:05.060 --> 00:08:06.883
need to be manually analyzed.

00:08:06.883 --> 00:08:09.050
As you can imagine, that's
extremely time-consuming,

00:08:09.050 --> 00:08:12.230
so you end up having
folks with PhDs in biology

00:08:12.230 --> 00:08:13.760
going through each
of these camera

00:08:13.760 --> 00:08:16.742
trap images individually
over a period of months.

00:08:16.742 --> 00:08:18.200
And what they're
working towards is

00:08:18.200 --> 00:08:22.570
reducing that time of analysis
from months to just days.

00:08:22.570 --> 00:08:24.980
And I'd like to share a
video that tells a bit more

00:08:24.980 --> 00:08:27.020
about ZSL and their journey.

00:08:34.772 --> 00:08:36.230
SOPHIE MAXWELL:
The main challenges

00:08:36.230 --> 00:08:39.890
facing wildlife today is
habitat loss, climate change,

00:08:39.890 --> 00:08:43.110
and threats from
illegal wildlife trade.

00:08:43.110 --> 00:08:46.700
ZSL's mission is to create a
world where wildlife thrives.

00:08:46.700 --> 00:08:49.190
We work in over 50
countries around the world

00:08:49.190 --> 00:08:50.880
where we empower
and inform people,

00:08:50.880 --> 00:08:53.780
to help stop wild
animals going extinct

00:08:53.780 --> 00:08:55.850
we have been working
with Google to look

00:08:55.850 --> 00:09:00.310
at how we can use Cloud AutoML
to really make a difference.

00:09:00.310 --> 00:09:03.080
OLIVER WEARN: We use
camera traps to monitor

00:09:03.080 --> 00:09:04.440
the state of the planet.

00:09:04.440 --> 00:09:06.740
They are tiny little
digital cameras

00:09:06.740 --> 00:09:09.300
hooked up to a little
motion sensor, basically.

00:09:09.300 --> 00:09:11.300
So, when an animal
walks past, they

00:09:11.300 --> 00:09:14.680
snap a photo, which you can
then retrieve at a later time.

00:09:14.680 --> 00:09:17.840
As well as telling you about how
abundant different species are,

00:09:17.840 --> 00:09:20.390
they can also offer
behavioral insights as well.

00:09:20.390 --> 00:09:22.250
ELISSE ROCHE: One
camera trap study

00:09:22.250 --> 00:09:24.500
generates hundreds of
thousands of images.

00:09:24.500 --> 00:09:27.200
So, over six months, you might
get half a million images

00:09:27.200 --> 00:09:29.060
that you need to sort
through and analyze.

00:09:29.060 --> 00:09:31.477
SAM SECCOMBE: You've got to
go through every single image.

00:09:31.477 --> 00:09:33.890
You've got a label them--
the time, the date, what it

00:09:33.890 --> 00:09:35.720
contains, which camera pulls.

00:09:35.720 --> 00:09:37.185
At the moment, it takes months.

00:09:37.185 --> 00:09:40.700
SOPHIE MAXWELL: If we can speed
up the analysis from six months

00:09:40.700 --> 00:09:42.725
to just days,
that's revolutionary

00:09:42.725 --> 00:09:45.170
in our conservation effort.

00:09:45.170 --> 00:09:48.290
Google Cloud AutoML
conservationists

00:09:48.290 --> 00:09:51.030
who are non-coders to
use machine-learning

00:09:51.030 --> 00:09:54.230
to create image recognition
models to identify species

00:09:54.230 --> 00:09:55.835
in camera tracking images.

00:09:55.835 --> 00:09:58.630
OLIVER WEARN: These are
images that I have labeled,

00:09:58.630 --> 00:10:02.480
and they're going to teach
the AutoML algorithm what

00:10:02.480 --> 00:10:03.765
different species look like.

00:10:03.765 --> 00:10:06.390
SAM SECCOMBE: If you can
just AutoML it, you can go--

00:10:06.390 --> 00:10:07.940
show me the images
that are empty,

00:10:07.940 --> 00:10:10.570
show me the images that have the
animal that I'm interested in.

00:10:10.570 --> 00:10:12.528
You could actually use
your scientific research

00:10:12.528 --> 00:10:14.570
and conservation time to
do conservation rather

00:10:14.570 --> 00:10:16.920
than doing data analysis.

00:10:16.920 --> 00:10:19.880
SOPHIE MAXWELL: We might use
the data to actually influence

00:10:19.880 --> 00:10:22.435
policy change or
conservation action.

00:10:22.435 --> 00:10:24.740
AI is going to play
a really big part

00:10:24.740 --> 00:10:26.870
in collecting all
the data together

00:10:26.870 --> 00:10:29.730
from wildlife conservation
sites around the world.

00:10:29.730 --> 00:10:31.400
And, if we do that
in real time, we

00:10:31.400 --> 00:10:34.010
can use AutoML to really
monitor the pulse of wildlife

00:10:34.010 --> 00:10:34.760
across the planet.

00:10:56.452 --> 00:10:58.872
ALONSO ORTIZ GALAN: So,
before I talk about the work

00:10:58.872 --> 00:11:01.330
we do at the Global Partnership
for Sustainable Development

00:11:01.330 --> 00:11:04.760
Data, I want to talk about
the 17 sustainable development

00:11:04.760 --> 00:11:05.260
goals.

00:11:09.204 --> 00:11:11.300
What you're seeing
in this light is

00:11:11.300 --> 00:11:13.370
the result of years
of negotiations

00:11:13.370 --> 00:11:16.040
between 193 countries.

00:11:16.040 --> 00:11:20.820
They were agreed in 2015, and
they are to be met in 2030.

00:11:20.820 --> 00:11:24.290
They are a framework or
a guide to take action

00:11:24.290 --> 00:11:27.530
towards reducing poverty,
protecting the planet,

00:11:27.530 --> 00:11:31.330
ensuring prosperous societies.

00:11:31.330 --> 00:11:36.080
And I want to highlight for
it key things about the SDGs.

00:11:36.080 --> 00:11:40.430
First, the fact that 193
countries agreed to something,

00:11:40.430 --> 00:11:43.190
especially such an
inspirational framework,

00:11:43.190 --> 00:11:46.040
successful in and of itself,
especially because they

00:11:46.040 --> 00:11:52.640
have been widely able to
capture attention from citizens,

00:11:52.640 --> 00:11:55.380
civil society, and companies.

00:11:55.380 --> 00:11:57.380
So, the second aspect
that I want to highlight

00:11:57.380 --> 00:11:59.630
is their universality.

00:11:59.630 --> 00:12:03.420
They're not only to be
implemented by governments,

00:12:03.420 --> 00:12:06.470
but also by other stakeholders.

00:12:06.470 --> 00:12:10.160
And, thankfully, many have
committed to achieving them

00:12:10.160 --> 00:12:12.620
and are adapting
and adopting them,

00:12:12.620 --> 00:12:17.330
whether from civil society
or from the private sector.

00:12:17.330 --> 00:12:18.710
And they're
contributing to them.

00:12:18.710 --> 00:12:21.200
And I also want to highlight
that they're not only

00:12:21.200 --> 00:12:23.070
to be implemented in
developing countries,

00:12:23.070 --> 00:12:25.230
but also in development
[INAUDIBLE]..

00:12:25.230 --> 00:12:27.080
The third thing I
want to highlight

00:12:27.080 --> 00:12:30.830
is that even though we're
seeing individual goals,

00:12:30.830 --> 00:12:32.930
they're all connected
to each other.

00:12:32.930 --> 00:12:35.720
In order to achieve
long-lasting change,

00:12:35.720 --> 00:12:38.510
we need to tackle all of
them at the same time.

00:12:38.510 --> 00:12:41.630
For example, SDG2,
ending hunger,

00:12:41.630 --> 00:12:45.430
is connected to SDG16 and SDG15.

00:12:45.430 --> 00:12:48.110
So, when thinking
about ending hunger,

00:12:48.110 --> 00:12:52.080
we need to think of land rights
and protecting the environment.

00:12:52.080 --> 00:12:54.130
So, they're connected
to each other.

00:12:54.130 --> 00:12:57.500
And, finally, the
architect of the SDGs--

00:12:57.500 --> 00:13:00.590
besides giving goals--
they also left us

00:13:00.590 --> 00:13:05.330
with 169 targets
and 233 indicators

00:13:05.330 --> 00:13:07.070
to accompany the agenda.

00:13:07.070 --> 00:13:10.100
And the targets can be
thought of as the objectives

00:13:10.100 --> 00:13:12.630
and the policies that
need to be put in place.

00:13:12.630 --> 00:13:14.120
And the indicators
are the way we

00:13:14.120 --> 00:13:16.790
measure progress along
the way and, ultimately,

00:13:16.790 --> 00:13:18.900
if we achieve impact.

00:13:18.900 --> 00:13:23.060
So, however, there's a big
a missing piece in disposal,

00:13:23.060 --> 00:13:24.680
and that's data.

00:13:24.680 --> 00:13:27.620
Data is the lifeblood of
sustainable development.

00:13:27.620 --> 00:13:29.840
And, in this case,
the responsibility

00:13:29.840 --> 00:13:33.920
falls mainly in governments
who are mandated to officially

00:13:33.920 --> 00:13:36.140
report on the SDGs.

00:13:36.140 --> 00:13:38.690
And, as we know,
governments have a hard time

00:13:38.690 --> 00:13:41.890
to produce timely
or real time data.

00:13:41.890 --> 00:13:46.220
In many cases, governments
are taking decisions

00:13:46.220 --> 00:13:48.680
with old data, or,
in some cases--

00:13:48.680 --> 00:13:53.120
in the case of countries
afflicted by conflict,

00:13:53.120 --> 00:13:54.530
there is no data at all.

00:13:54.530 --> 00:13:59.940
So, that's where the work of
the Global Partnership comes in.

00:13:59.940 --> 00:14:03.090
We're a global network
committed to ensure

00:14:03.090 --> 00:14:06.260
we have the necessary data to
achieve and monitor the SDGs.

00:14:06.260 --> 00:14:09.990
We were founded in 2015
alongside the sustainable

00:14:09.990 --> 00:14:12.360
development goals,
and we bring together

00:14:12.360 --> 00:14:16.200
governments, civil society,
UN agencies, multilaterals,

00:14:16.200 --> 00:14:19.020
and the private sector
to collectively solve

00:14:19.020 --> 00:14:23.842
the problems and gaps around
the production and use of data.

00:14:23.842 --> 00:14:25.800
We don't collect or
provide the data ourselves,

00:14:25.800 --> 00:14:29.760
we'd rather provide strategic
partnerships, advocacy,

00:14:29.760 --> 00:14:33.180
technical assistance, and
pilot tests for our partners

00:14:33.180 --> 00:14:36.860
at the national, regional,
and global level.

00:14:36.860 --> 00:14:41.480
We also provide offline and
online spaces for partners

00:14:41.480 --> 00:14:44.460
to come together and
tackle specific challenges.

00:14:44.460 --> 00:14:48.260
For example, we set up
a SDG data collaborative

00:14:48.260 --> 00:14:51.500
with UN statistics deviation
to work towards better

00:14:51.500 --> 00:14:55.550
interoperability, and we
host international events

00:14:55.550 --> 00:14:58.480
such as a Data for
Development Festival.

00:14:58.480 --> 00:15:02.260
Finally, all the work
that we do attempts

00:15:02.260 --> 00:15:05.110
to reduce the barriers-- the
technical barriers that there

00:15:05.110 --> 00:15:09.260
are to have better data,
but also the political ones.

00:15:09.260 --> 00:15:15.650
For example, I want to talk
about the Africa regional data.

00:15:15.650 --> 00:15:19.000
And so, we're supporting five
countries in East and West

00:15:19.000 --> 00:15:22.720
Africa to access
and use 17 years

00:15:22.720 --> 00:15:27.340
of satellite data in order to
improve their policy-making.

00:15:27.340 --> 00:15:31.060
And we are connecting them
with key partners such as NASA

00:15:31.060 --> 00:15:33.010
and the Group on
Earth observations

00:15:33.010 --> 00:15:36.130
so they can have
analysis and ready data

00:15:36.130 --> 00:15:38.820
through an infrastructure
called a datacube.

00:15:38.820 --> 00:15:42.670
The datacube has
many applications.

00:15:42.670 --> 00:15:47.920
It helps governments
understand land degradation,

00:15:47.920 --> 00:15:52.850
detect illegal binding, and
strengthen food security.

00:15:52.850 --> 00:15:55.720
So, about a decade ago,
a country such as Kenya

00:15:55.720 --> 00:15:59.170
would have paid approximately
$1 million per year

00:15:59.170 --> 00:16:00.780
in order to access these data.

00:16:00.780 --> 00:16:04.990
Harder, it is now open and
free, but the challenge

00:16:04.990 --> 00:16:07.040
does not end with having data.

00:16:07.040 --> 00:16:10.150
There are many
technical issues--

00:16:10.150 --> 00:16:12.280
whole infrastructures
that need to be set up

00:16:12.280 --> 00:16:14.740
in order to collect,
have the data,

00:16:14.740 --> 00:16:18.950
and analyze it, and have the
necessary capacities to do so.

00:16:18.950 --> 00:16:21.580
So, by bringing together
these five countries

00:16:21.580 --> 00:16:23.210
through a common
infrastructure will

00:16:23.210 --> 00:16:27.640
help them to troubleshoot
together, to take training

00:16:27.640 --> 00:16:31.240
together, and as well to learn
from each other along the way.

00:16:31.240 --> 00:16:34.810
And especially
having governments

00:16:34.810 --> 00:16:38.390
at the highest level agree and
commit to this infrastructure,

00:16:38.390 --> 00:16:41.120
we're also putting the
right political attention

00:16:41.120 --> 00:16:44.350
so it has resources, and
there's collaboration

00:16:44.350 --> 00:16:46.960
between different
agencies and ministries.

00:16:46.960 --> 00:16:50.850
And we are expanding
this initiative

00:16:50.850 --> 00:16:54.040
to support countries in Latin
America and the Caribbean.

00:16:54.040 --> 00:16:58.000
And we're doing so with
the help of Google Cloud

00:16:58.000 --> 00:17:01.180
and some credits that we're
getting through their Data

00:17:01.180 --> 00:17:03.435
Solution for Change program.

00:17:03.435 --> 00:17:04.810
And, in addition
to that, we have

00:17:04.810 --> 00:17:09.550
partnered for the last
year with Google Cloud

00:17:09.550 --> 00:17:13.030
to do a series of
SDG-related contests

00:17:13.030 --> 00:17:16.450
in which we want
to get better ideas

00:17:16.450 --> 00:17:21.121
and have attention on how to
bring solutions for the SDGs.

00:17:23.947 --> 00:17:25.970
ELISSE ROCHE: Thanks, Alonso.

00:17:25.970 --> 00:17:28.960
So, one of the SDG-related
contests that Alonso just

00:17:28.960 --> 00:17:32.550
mentioned is Visualize 2030.

00:17:32.550 --> 00:17:35.430
Visualize 2030-- data
stories for the SDGs--

00:17:35.430 --> 00:17:38.280
is a data storytelling
contest for students

00:17:38.280 --> 00:17:40.440
where we are students
from around the world

00:17:40.440 --> 00:17:43.080
to analyze data sets
from the World Bank

00:17:43.080 --> 00:17:45.510
and from the United
Nations Statistics Division

00:17:45.510 --> 00:17:48.150
to visualize that data
using Google Data Studio

00:17:48.150 --> 00:17:51.000
and to, ultimately, tell
a data-driven story about

00:17:51.000 --> 00:17:53.370
how at least two of the
Sustainable Development Goals

00:17:53.370 --> 00:17:57.360
influence each other and how
we can achieve them by 2030.

00:17:57.360 --> 00:18:00.630
There's an interesting backstory
behind this contest as well.

00:18:00.630 --> 00:18:03.330
So, Alonso mentioned the Data
for Development Festival,

00:18:03.330 --> 00:18:06.450
before and that was, in
part, the inspiration

00:18:06.450 --> 00:18:09.180
for the Visualize 2030 contest.

00:18:09.180 --> 00:18:10.980
At that festival,
there were a lot

00:18:10.980 --> 00:18:14.190
of talks in sessions about the
importance of democratizing

00:18:14.190 --> 00:18:17.640
access to data, about
inviting everyone to the table

00:18:17.640 --> 00:18:21.660
and making this data accessible
and consumable by everyone.

00:18:21.660 --> 00:18:24.810
And data visualization has
a big part to play in that.

00:18:24.810 --> 00:18:27.450
And that's essentially
what led to the ideation

00:18:27.450 --> 00:18:30.960
around this contest and bringing
it to life later that year

00:18:30.960 --> 00:18:33.110
in 2018.

00:18:33.110 --> 00:18:36.300
For the applicants, the top
five eligible submissions

00:18:36.300 --> 00:18:39.720
were to receive a $10,000
cash prize, an announcement,

00:18:39.720 --> 00:18:41.430
and promotion by Google Cloud.

00:18:44.060 --> 00:18:47.150
We also were working with
the World Bank and also

00:18:47.150 --> 00:18:50.182
the United Nations Foundation
to make this possible.

00:18:50.182 --> 00:18:52.640
So, again, thinking about what's
at the heart of the Global

00:18:52.640 --> 00:18:54.890
Partnership for sustainable
development data-- there's

00:18:54.890 --> 00:18:57.200
this idea of cooperation
and building bridges.

00:18:57.200 --> 00:18:59.810
And this is definitely also
at the heart of this contest.

00:19:02.720 --> 00:19:05.390
We also worked with an
expert panel of judges

00:19:05.390 --> 00:19:09.200
in order to select the final
five winners, including

00:19:09.200 --> 00:19:11.330
Rajesh Mirchandani
chief communications

00:19:11.330 --> 00:19:13.730
officer of the United
Nations Foundation,

00:19:13.730 --> 00:19:17.190
Claire Melamed, who is the CEO
of the Global Partnership team,

00:19:17.190 --> 00:19:19.970
Haishan Fu, a director
at the World Bank,

00:19:19.970 --> 00:19:22.700
Richard Curtis, who is a
writer-director and co-founder

00:19:22.700 --> 00:19:23.780
of the Red Nose Day.

00:19:23.780 --> 00:19:26.420
You also may be familiar with
some of his popular films

00:19:26.420 --> 00:19:28.520
like "Love Actually."

00:19:28.520 --> 00:19:32.160
Cindi Howson, who, at the
time, was a VP at Gartner,

00:19:32.160 --> 00:19:34.790
Fausto Ibarra, director
of product management

00:19:34.790 --> 00:19:38.150
with Google Marketing
platform, and Simon Rogers--

00:19:38.150 --> 00:19:39.840
data editor at the
News Lab at Google.

00:19:39.840 --> 00:19:41.250
So, with this expert
panel of judges,

00:19:41.250 --> 00:19:42.917
we were really looking
to bring together

00:19:42.917 --> 00:19:46.190
variety of expertise areas
together from sustainable

00:19:46.190 --> 00:19:49.430
development to communications
to data storytelling

00:19:49.430 --> 00:19:52.400
in order to assess the
finalists and determine, again,

00:19:52.400 --> 00:19:53.390
these top five winners.

00:19:56.862 --> 00:19:59.040
ALONSO ORTIZ GALAN: So,
these are the five students

00:19:59.040 --> 00:20:02.850
who submitted the most
compelling SDG data stories.

00:20:02.850 --> 00:20:07.220
Actually, with us, we have
Evan, Kelsey, and Sarah.

00:20:07.220 --> 00:20:09.653
Could you please
raise your hands?

00:20:09.653 --> 00:20:10.820
Just a round of applause me.

00:20:10.820 --> 00:20:14.354
[APPLAUSE]

00:20:17.780 --> 00:20:19.520
They come from
different backgrounds,

00:20:19.520 --> 00:20:21.830
are specializing
different topics,

00:20:21.830 --> 00:20:24.290
and some are doing
undergraduate studies

00:20:24.290 --> 00:20:26.210
and some are graduate students.

00:20:26.210 --> 00:20:29.120
However, through
storytelling and design,

00:20:29.120 --> 00:20:33.260
they all provided
compelling ways and stories

00:20:33.260 --> 00:20:35.720
in which to talk about the SDGs.

00:20:35.720 --> 00:20:39.590
And their ideas could
have real world impact,

00:20:39.590 --> 00:20:41.870
and we hope they
don't stop here.

00:20:41.870 --> 00:20:44.660
What stood out amongst
all of the submissions--

00:20:44.660 --> 00:20:47.150
the winning submissions--
is that they also

00:20:47.150 --> 00:20:49.370
found these
relationships between how

00:20:49.370 --> 00:20:51.230
one goal affects the other.

00:20:51.230 --> 00:20:55.480
And positive changes in one
to benefits on others and vice

00:20:55.480 --> 00:20:55.980
versa.

00:21:00.690 --> 00:21:04.340
So, Anisa is a master's student
of interactive journalism

00:21:04.340 --> 00:21:06.590
at City University of London.

00:21:06.590 --> 00:21:10.670
She focused on SDG5
and SDG16 which

00:21:10.670 --> 00:21:13.050
are gender equality,
peace, justice,

00:21:13.050 --> 00:21:15.680
and strong institutions
respectively.

00:21:15.680 --> 00:21:18.140
She explored the relationship
between increased

00:21:18.140 --> 00:21:21.200
female participation and
less corruption and stronger

00:21:21.200 --> 00:21:22.650
institutions.

00:21:22.650 --> 00:21:26.870
She zoomed in Rwanda's data as
an example of positive change.

00:21:31.950 --> 00:21:35.040
So, Anna is a student
at UC-Berkeley's master

00:21:35.040 --> 00:21:38.250
of information and
data science program.

00:21:38.250 --> 00:21:43.980
She focused on SDG3,
SDG4, SDG5, and SDG8,

00:21:43.980 --> 00:21:47.680
which are good home and
well-being, quality education,

00:21:47.680 --> 00:21:51.120
gender equality, decent
work, and economic growth,

00:21:51.120 --> 00:21:52.590
respectively.

00:21:52.590 --> 00:21:55.710
She explores these
dimensions across a variety

00:21:55.710 --> 00:21:59.730
of civilizations to make the
case that gender equality must

00:21:59.730 --> 00:22:03.600
be embedded in core social
structures to have an impact.

00:22:03.600 --> 00:22:05.880
And that, as a
result, other areas

00:22:05.880 --> 00:22:08.280
that rely on these
same structures

00:22:08.280 --> 00:22:09.780
will have positive gains.

00:22:14.090 --> 00:22:18.520
Evan is a commerce student
at McMaster University.

00:22:18.520 --> 00:22:20.520
He focused on SDG1--

00:22:20.520 --> 00:22:22.990
no poverty-- and SDG13--

00:22:22.990 --> 00:22:24.730
climate action.

00:22:24.730 --> 00:22:27.070
He walked us through
the story of how,

00:22:27.070 --> 00:22:30.250
as middle countries
begin to catch up

00:22:30.250 --> 00:22:34.240
with high income states,
the resource, consumption,

00:22:34.240 --> 00:22:38.780
and CO2 contributions
exponentially increase.

00:22:38.780 --> 00:22:41.750
He used data to back
up his recommendations

00:22:41.750 --> 00:22:45.820
and provided useful ways
to engage on these issues.

00:22:49.159 --> 00:22:52.600
ELISSE ROCHE: So, another one
of the finalists, Kelsey Wang,

00:22:52.600 --> 00:22:56.050
developed a data story called
"The Inequalities of Today

00:22:56.050 --> 00:22:58.420
and Tomorrow" where
she focused on how

00:22:58.420 --> 00:23:01.180
education can help to address
both poverty and climate

00:23:01.180 --> 00:23:02.110
change--

00:23:02.110 --> 00:23:05.920
looking at SDGs 1, 4, and 13.

00:23:05.920 --> 00:23:08.200
She began her data
story with a question--

00:23:08.200 --> 00:23:10.940
are people doing better
or worse than before?

00:23:10.940 --> 00:23:13.180
How can we compare the
past to the present?

00:23:13.180 --> 00:23:15.170
A very loaded
question, of course.

00:23:15.170 --> 00:23:17.470
But, from there, she dug
deeper into the expansion

00:23:17.470 --> 00:23:19.990
of the human population
which, as of 2017,

00:23:19.990 --> 00:23:21.480
is over 7 billion people--

00:23:21.480 --> 00:23:23.830
and also changes
in life expectancy.

00:23:23.830 --> 00:23:25.720
And, looking at
those two variables

00:23:25.720 --> 00:23:28.450
to think about the
unintended consequences

00:23:28.450 --> 00:23:29.840
that come from that--

00:23:29.840 --> 00:23:34.570
for example, industrial
agriculture and land

00:23:34.570 --> 00:23:37.040
scarcity as well.

00:23:37.040 --> 00:23:39.760
Especially with increases
in income inequality,

00:23:39.760 --> 00:23:42.460
Kelsey made the case that
education and internet

00:23:42.460 --> 00:23:46.090
technologies can actually act
as an equalizer across the world

00:23:46.090 --> 00:23:48.460
and also serve to
empower individuals

00:23:48.460 --> 00:23:52.120
around how they can take
action around climate change.

00:23:52.120 --> 00:23:54.160
At the end of her
story, Kelsey concluded

00:23:54.160 --> 00:23:55.960
that quality
education for everyone

00:23:55.960 --> 00:23:59.047
is a building block for a
better, more sustainable,

00:23:59.047 --> 00:24:00.130
and more equitable future.

00:24:03.650 --> 00:24:06.320
Sarah Liou, in her
data story titled,

00:24:06.320 --> 00:24:08.990
"Earth, Water, Air, and
Rising Temperatures"

00:24:08.990 --> 00:24:11.240
focused on a total
of four SDGs--

00:24:11.240 --> 00:24:13.270
so not just two, but four.

00:24:13.270 --> 00:24:17.690
Specifically SDG2 on zero
hunger, SDG6, clean water

00:24:17.690 --> 00:24:21.560
and sanitation, SDG13
around climate action,

00:24:21.560 --> 00:24:24.790
and SDG15, life on land.

00:24:24.790 --> 00:24:26.680
So, as you can
see pictured here,

00:24:26.680 --> 00:24:29.240
Sarah developed an
interactive roadmap

00:24:29.240 --> 00:24:32.150
to sustainable development,
really analyzing the effects

00:24:32.150 --> 00:24:34.700
that agriculture has upon
the environment from land

00:24:34.700 --> 00:24:36.860
use to water scarcity.

00:24:36.860 --> 00:24:39.410
She assessed agricultural
greenhouse gas emissions

00:24:39.410 --> 00:24:43.158
over time, changes in land
use and also livestock, waste

00:24:43.158 --> 00:24:45.200
management-- so really
looking at these variables

00:24:45.200 --> 00:24:46.460
holistically.

00:24:46.460 --> 00:24:49.610
In addition, she presented a
case study on Madagascar land

00:24:49.610 --> 00:24:52.250
use and how nearly
800 mammal species

00:24:52.250 --> 00:24:55.610
are threatened by agriculture
and habitat loss today.

00:24:55.610 --> 00:24:59.870
Another case study
explored in this data story

00:24:59.870 --> 00:25:04.130
was that of Ethiopia,
where in 2016, over 90%

00:25:04.130 --> 00:25:07.850
of available fresh water was
used for agricultural purposes.

00:25:07.850 --> 00:25:10.610
Overall, Sarah made the case
for sustainable agriculture

00:25:10.610 --> 00:25:14.390
as key to combating climate
change, fostering life on land,

00:25:14.390 --> 00:25:16.940
providing clean water and
sanitation to everyone,

00:25:16.940 --> 00:25:19.310
and concluded with a call
to action for everyone

00:25:19.310 --> 00:25:21.150
to practice more
sustainable habits,

00:25:21.150 --> 00:25:23.120
including limiting
meat consumption

00:25:23.120 --> 00:25:25.010
and supporting urban farming.

00:25:28.000 --> 00:25:30.700
So, with that, we would like
to say there is an opportunity

00:25:30.700 --> 00:25:33.190
to learn more about our
respective organizations

00:25:33.190 --> 00:25:35.320
and these initiatives,
starting with the Global

00:25:35.320 --> 00:25:37.950
Partnership for Sustainable
Development Data.

